Abstract

Modern electric vehicles are nonlinear multiphysics systems in which electrical, thermal, mechanical, aerodynamic, and cabin subsystems interact over multiple timescales. Integrated optimal control of such systems is an established research field. The present work does not claim novelty for integrated control, subsystem coupling, decomposition, or optimal-control sensitivity analysis in general.

This work studies a narrower mathematical object: the mission-level optimal-control value change associated with removing one physically identified coupling from a nonlinear constrained electric-vehicle model. Let the complete system dynamics be

\dot{x}=f(x,u,w),

and let e denote a specific cross-domain physical coupling. Removing e produces a structured model perturbation

\tilde f_e(x,u,w)=f(x,u,w)-\Delta_e(x,u,w).

The perturbation \Delta_e is block-sparse in the state partition induced by the physical domains.

For a finite mission horizon H, let J^*(H) denote the optimal value of the full model and J_e^*(H) the optimal value of the reduced model. Define the signed coupling-removal value

V_e(H):=J_e^*(H)-J^*(H).

Koltai and Junge [1] show that for weakly coupled LQ systems, a coupling of arbitrarily small strength can destabilize the closed loop formed by the uncoupled optimal feedback, and that the value function may then change drastically. The correct research object is therefore |V_e(H)|.

The principal mathematical objective is to determine whether existing optimal-control sensitivity theory can yield a useful structured bound

|V_e(H)| \leq \mathcal B_e(H),

where \mathcal B_e(H) depends explicitly on the magnitude, direction, and dynamical propagation of the removed physical coupling. The first-order sensitivity is governed by the costate:

V_e(H)-V(0)
=
-\int_0^H p^*(t)^T \Delta_e(x^*(t),u^*(t),w(t))\,dt
+
O(\|\Delta_e\|_{G}^2),

where V(0):=J^*(H) and \|\cdot\|_G is the Banach-space norm of the component function, under the Carathéodory-type assumptions of Cangelosi and Heinkenschloss [2]. Their framework establishes continuous Fréchet differentiability via the Implicit Function Theorem applied to a Nemytskii-operator formulation.

The framework distinguishes the interior regime (m_g>0) from the active-constraint regime (m_g=0). In the active-set regime, Stechlinski [3] characterizes sensitivity jumps via lexicographic directional differentiation. Bettiol, Frankowska, and Vinter [4] interpret the costate as a Clarke subgradient of the value function. Submodularity of V_H(S) is posed as an open diagnostic; LQR/LQG actuator-selection objectives admit approximate supermodularity [5].

No experimental result is claimed.

