1. Epistemic Position and Collision Audit

This work is theoretical. The prior-art landscape imposes a precise collision audit.

Cangelosi–Heinkenschloss [2]. Their 2025 paper establishes sensitivities of the solution of an optimal control problem and a quantity of interest to perturbations in a state/control-dependent component function appearing in the ODEs and the objective. With Carathéodory-type assumptions, the Implicit Function Theorem is applied to a Nemytskii operator to establish continuous Fréchet differentiability. Their estimates for QoI change are sharper than classical Grönwall bounds.

Classical adjoint sensitivity. The first-order sensitivity of the optimal value with respect to a dynamics perturbation is the envelope/adjoint formula. Büskens and Maurer [6] develop sensitivity analysis for parametric nonlinear optimal control with control and state constraints.

Koltai–Junge [1]. For weakly coupled LQ problems, a "coupling-adapted" norm is proposed. Their main result: if a weak coupling suffices to destabilize the closed loop with the uncoupled optimal feedback, the value function may change drastically.

Active-set sensitivity. Stechlinski [3] establishes theory for optimization-constrained differential equations with active-set changes, under linear independence of constraints and strong second-order sufficiency. Sensitivity jumps are characterized via lexicographic directional differentiation.

State-constrained sensitivity. Bettiol, Frankowska, and Vinter [4] establish full and partial sensitivity relations for state-constrained problems, interpreting the costate as a Clarke subgradient of the value function with respect to the state, and the couple (costate, Hamiltonian) as the Clarke subgradient with respect to time and state.

Submodularity in control. LQR/LQG actuator-selection objectives fail to be exactly sub- or supermodular, but admit approximate supermodularity with near-optimality certificates [5].

The collision risk is high. The first-order formula is not new. Novelty must come from block-sparse specialization, active-set jumps, or submodularity diagnostics.

