2. Research Question

Consider domains \mathcal D = \{B,M,T,C,V,\ldots\}. Let E be the set of physically meaningful cross-domain couplings. The central question is: How does the optimal mission value change when a specified coupling e is removed? This is narrower than asking whether EV subsystems should be integrated.

---

3. Function Spaces and Standing Assumptions

State and control spaces. Let x\in W^{1,\infty}([0,H];\mathbb R^n) and u\in L^\infty([0,H];\mathbb R^m).

Component-function space. Let G = W^{k,\infty}(\mathcal R(H)\times\mathcal U\times\mathcal W;\mathbb R^n) denote the Banach space of component functions, with k chosen so that the Nemytskii operator is continuously Fréchet differentiable.

Carathéodory assumptions (Cangelosi–Heinkenschloss [2], Assumptions 2.1).

(C1) f(t,x,g) is measurable in t for each (x,g) and continuous in (x,g) for a.a. t.

(C2) There exists l_f\in L^1(0,H) such that for a.a. t,

\|f(t,x_1,g_1)-f(t,x_2,g_2)\|
\le l_f(t)\bigl(\|x_1-x_2\|+\|g_1-g_2\|\bigr).

(C3) There exists m_f\in L^\infty(0,H) such that \|f(t,0,0)\|\le m_f(t) for a.a. t.

Reachable tube. For the nominal optimal trajectory x^*(\cdot), define

\mathcal R(H) = \{x^*(t) + B_\rho : t\in[0,H]\},

with \rho>0 chosen so that \mathcal R(H) lies in the domain where (C1)–(C3) hold.

Two coupling norms. Define the trajectory norm

\kappa_e^{\text{traj}} = \sup_{t\in[0,H]}\|\Delta_e(x^*(t),u^*(t),w(t))\|

and the tube norm

\kappa_e^{\text{tube}} = \sup_{(x,u,w)\in\mathcal R(H)\times\mathcal U\times\mathcal W}\|\Delta_e(x,u,w)\|.

The first-order term uses \kappa_e^{\text{traj}}; the remainder uses \kappa_e^{\text{tube}}. This distinction is essential: the Fréchet derivative is continuous with respect to \|\cdot\|_G, not pointwise trajectory evaluation.

First-order optimality system. Let z=(x,u,p) and let

\psi(z,g)=0

denote the optimality conditions in Nemytskii form. The IFT requires bijectivity of

\psi_z(\bar z,\bar g).

---

4. Structured Coupling Removal

Partition x=(x_B,x_M,x_T,x_C,x_V). For a coupling e,

f(x,u,w)=f_{-e}(x,u,w)+\Delta_e(x,u,w).

The perturbation is block-sparse. For battery–thermal coupling,

\Delta_{BT}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\Delta f_B^{BT}\\
0\\
\Delta f_T^{BT}\\
0\\
0
\end{bmatrix}.

Definition 4.1 (Affected-domain projection). Let I_e\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\} be the set of state indices affected by coupling e. Define \Pi_e:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^{|I_e|} by \Pi_e x = x_{I_e}. Define the reduced costate

p_e^* := \Pi_e p^*.

The sharp adjoint integral is

P_e^{\text{adj}}(H)
=
\int_0^H \|p_e^*(t)\|\,\|\Delta_e(x^*(t),u^*(t),w(t))\|\,dt.

---

5. Signed Coupling-Removal Value

Define V_e(H):=J_e^*(H)-J^*(H).

Proposition 5.1 (Sign-indefiniteness). The inequality V_e(H)\ge 0 is not generally valid.

Proof. Take f_{-e}=u, \Delta_e=\mathrm{const}, x(0)=0, x(H)=\Delta_e H. The full model is feasible with u=0, giving J^*=0. The reduced model requires u=\Delta_e, giving J_e^*=\Delta_e^2 H. Therefore V_e=\Delta_e^2 H>0. Reversing the roles of full and reduced models yields V_e<0. \square

More fundamentally, Koltai and Junge [1] show that weak coupling can destabilize the uncoupled closed loop. Their result is the mathematically stronger justification for sign-indefiniteness.

---

6. Adjoint Sensitivity: The First-Order Object

Let f_\varepsilon=f-\varepsilon\Delta_e and V(\varepsilon)=\inf_u J_{f_\varepsilon}(u). Under interiority m_g>0, the second-order sufficient condition, and bijectivity of \psi_z,

V_e(H)-V(0)
=
-\int_0^H p^*(t)^T \Delta_e(x^*,u^*,w)\,dt
+
O(\kappa_e^{\text{tube}\,2}).

Remainder estimate. There exists C_R>0, depending on L_{zz} (Lipschitz constant of the Hessian of the Hamiltonian), \|\psi_z^{-1}\|, and the tube geometry, such that

R_e
\le
C_R\,\kappa_e^{\text{tube}\,2}.

Collision status. This formula is classical. Novelty lies only in the block-sparse specialization.

