7. Minimal Analytical Laboratory

For the two-subsystem system

\dot x_1=f_1(x_1,u_1)+\varepsilon q_1(x_1,x_2),\qquad
\dot x_2=f_2(x_2,u_2)+\varepsilon q_2(x_1,x_2),

the exact derivative is

\frac{\partial J^*}{\partial\varepsilon}
=
-\int_0^H p_1^{*T} q_1\,dt
-
\int_0^H p_2^{*T} q_2\,dt.

The second derivative \partial^2 J^*/\partial\varepsilon^2 governs curvature. If curvature is large, the first-order bound is unreliable even for small \varepsilon.

---

8. Interior-Regime Structured Bound

Assume (C1)–(C3), m_g>0, SSOSC, LICQ, and bijectivity of \psi_z. Then

|V_e(H)|
\le
\int_0^H\|p_e^*(t)\|\,\|\Delta_e(x^*,u^*,w)\|\,dt
+
C_R\,\kappa_e^{\text{tube}\,2}.

Candidate Theorem 1 (Structured constant). The research hypothesis is that there exists \gamma_e<1, depending on |I_e|/n and the block-sparsity pattern, such that

C_{\text{structured}} \le \gamma_e\,C_{\text{generic}}.

Establishing this requires:

(A7) Block-diagonal dominance. The linearized Jacobian A(t)=\partial_x f(x^*,u^*,w) is block-diagonal dominant in the coupling-adapted norm.

(A8) Separation condition. There exists s_0>0 such that the spectrum of the closed-loop block i is separated from the spectrum of block j by at least s_0 for all i\neq j.

Under (A7)–(A8), a Koltai–Junge-type Lemma 5 estimate applied to the linearized LQOCP gives

\|p_{\text{complement}}\|
\le
\frac{1}{s_0}\,\|p_e^*\|,

yielding \gamma_e = |I_e|/(n\,s_0). Without (A7)–(A8), Candidate Theorem 1 remains a conjecture.

---

9. Active-Constraint Regime

When m_g=0, the classical adjoint formula fails.

Control constraints only. If g(u)\le 0 is active, the multiplier \mu\in L^\infty. Stechlinski's lexicographic directional differentiation applies directly under LICQ and SSOSC. The sensitivity jump is characterized by an auxiliary nonsmooth sensitivity system.

State constraints. If g(x)\le 0 is active, the multiplier is a measure supported on the active set \mathcal A. Bettiol–Frankowska–Vinter interpret the costate as a Clarke subgradient:

p(t)\in \partial_C V(t,x^*(t)).

The directional derivative of the value function is not a single integral but a min–max over the Clarke subdifferential:

DV_e(H;\Delta_e)
=
\min_{\mu\in\mathcal M}\max_{p\in\partial_C V}
\left[
\int_{\mathcal A}\mu^T\nabla_x g\,\delta x_e\,dt
+
\int_{\mathcal A}p^T\Delta_e\,dt
\right],

where \delta x_e solves the linearized dynamics. The projection of \Delta_e onto the normal cone of the active constraint determines whether the active set expands or shrinks.

Research gap. A quantitative jump estimate specialized to block-sparse \Delta_e, expressed in the Clarke framework, is open.

---

10. Horizon-Dependent Coupling Value

Lemma 10.1 (Non-monotonicity). Assume L\ge 0, free terminal state, and no terminal constraints. Then J^*(H) is monotone nondecreasing in H. However, V_e(H) is not necessarily monotone in H.

Proof. Monotonicity of J^* follows by extending the control with u=0 on [H,H']. Non-monotonicity of V_e follows because increasing H may activate a constraint inactive at smaller horizons, changing the sign of the first-order term. \square

Koltai–Junge's estimates are horizon-dependent through the coupled Riccati equations.

