APPENDICES

Appendix A: Banach-Space Framework and Carathéodory Assumptions

A.1 Function Spaces

Let I = (0,H). Define the state space

X := W^{1,\infty}(I;\mathbb{R}^n),

equipped with the norm \|x\|_X = \|x\|_{L^\infty} + \|\dot{x}\|_{L^\infty}. Let

U := L^\infty(I;\mathbb{R}^m),\qquad 
P := W^{1,\infty}(I;\mathbb{R}^n)

denote the control and costate spaces. The component-function space is

G := W^{k,\infty}(\mathcal{R}(H)\times\mathcal{U}\times\mathcal{W};\mathbb{R}^n),

with k chosen so that the Nemytskii operator defined below is continuously Fréchet differentiable. The optimality system space is

Z := X \times U \times P \times \mathbb{R}^{n_p},

where n_p is the number of parameters. The Banach space for the operator equation is

Y := L^\infty(I;\mathbb{R}^n) \times L^\infty(I;\mathbb{R}^m) \times L^\infty(I;\mathbb{R}^n) \times \mathbb{R}^{n_p}.

A.2 Carathéodory-Type Assumptions (C1)–(C3)

The following assumptions are imported verbatim from Cangelosi and Heinkenschloss [2, Assumptions 2.1].

(C1) Measurability and continuity. The function f:I\times\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}^{n_g}\to\mathbb{R}^n satisfies:

· f(t,x,g) is measurable in t for each (x,g);
· f(t,x,g) is continuous in (x,g) for a.a. t\in I.

(C2) Lipschitz condition. There exists l_f\in L^1(I) such that for a.a. t\in I and all (x_1,g_1),(x_2,g_2)\in\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}^{n_g},

\|f(t,x_1,g_1)-f(t,x_2,g_2)\| \le l_f(t)\bigl(\|x_1-x_2\|+\|g_1-g_2\|\bigr).

(C3) Integrable bound at origin. There exists m_f\in L^\infty(I) such that for a.a. t\in I,

\|f(t,0,0)\| \le m_f(t).

A.3 Nemytskii Operator and Optimality System

Define the Nemytskii (superposition) operator

\mathcal{N}_f: X\times G \to L^\infty(I;\mathbb{R}^n),\qquad
\mathcal{N}_f(x,g)(t) := f(t,x(t),g(x(t))).

Under (C1)–(C3), \mathcal{N}_f is well-defined and continuously Fréchet differentiable.

Let z = (x,u,p,\lambda)\in Z collect the state, control, costate, and parameters. The first-order optimality conditions of the optimal control problem can be written as an operator equation

\psi(z,g) = 0,

where \psi: Z\times G \to Y encodes:

· the state equation \dot{x} - \mathcal{N}_f(x,g) = 0;
· the costate equation -\dot{p} - \partial_x H(x,u,p,g) = 0;
· the stationarity condition \partial_u H(x,u,p,g) = 0;
· the boundary and transversality conditions.

The operator \psi is continuously Fréchet differentiable under (C1)–(C3) and standard smoothness of L and \Phi.

A.4 Bijectivity of \psi_z and the IFT

Let (\bar{z},\bar{g}) be a nominal solution of \psi(\bar{z},\bar{g})=0. The following assumption is required for the Implicit Function Theorem.

(B1) Bijectivity of the linearized operator. The partial Fréchet derivative

\psi_z(\bar{z},\bar{g}): Z \to Y

is a linear homeomorphism.

Under (C1)–(C3) and (B1), the Implicit Function Theorem yields a unique continuously Fréchet differentiable map

\mathcal{S}: G \to Z,\qquad \mathcal{S}(g) = z_g,

defined in a neighborhood of \bar{g}, such that \psi(\mathcal{S}(g),g)=0. The derivative is

D\mathcal{S}(\bar{g}) = -\psi_z(\bar{z},\bar{g})^{-1}\,\psi_g(\bar{z},\bar{g}).

This is the functional-analytic foundation for the first-order sensitivity formula in §6.

