Appendix B: Detailed Proof of the First-Order Sensitivity Formula

B.1 Setup

Let f_\varepsilon := f - \varepsilon\Delta_e, and let J^*(\varepsilon) denote the optimal value of the problem with dynamics f_\varepsilon. The nominal problem corresponds to \varepsilon=0 with value J^*(0)=J^*(H). The reduced problem corresponds to \varepsilon=1 with value J_e^*(H)=J^*(1).

B.2 Differentiation of the Optimality System

By the chain rule and the Implicit Function Theorem (Appendix A.4), the map \varepsilon\mapsto z_\varepsilon is continuously Fréchet differentiable in a neighborhood of \varepsilon=0. Differentiating \psi(z_\varepsilon,\varepsilon\Delta_e)=0 with respect to \varepsilon at \varepsilon=0 gives

\psi_z(\bar{z},\bar{g})\,\frac{dz}{d\varepsilon}\bigg|_{\varepsilon=0}
+
\psi_g(\bar{z},\bar{g})\,\Delta_e = 0.

Therefore

\frac{dz}{d\varepsilon}\bigg|_{\varepsilon=0}
=
-\psi_z(\bar{z},\bar{g})^{-1}\,\psi_g(\bar{z},\bar{g})\,\Delta_e.

B.3 Differentiation of the Objective

The optimal value is

J^*(\varepsilon) = \Phi(x_\varepsilon(H)) + \int_0^H L(x_\varepsilon,u_\varepsilon,w)\,dt.

Differentiating with respect to \varepsilon at \varepsilon=0 and using the stationarity of the nominal problem (the derivative of the Lagrangian with respect to z vanishes), only the explicit dependence on \varepsilon through f_\varepsilon survives. The envelope theorem gives

\frac{dJ^*}{d\varepsilon}\bigg|_{\varepsilon=0}
=
-\int_0^H p^*(t)^T \Delta_e(x^*(t),u^*(t),w(t))\,dt.

This is the classical adjoint formula. The modern functional-analytic justification is provided by Cangelosi and Heinkenschloss [2].

B.4 Remainder Estimate

By Taylor's theorem with integral remainder,

J^*(1) - J^*(0) = \frac{dJ^*}{d\varepsilon}\bigg|_{\varepsilon=0} + \int_0^1 (1-s) \frac{d^2J^*}{d\varepsilon^2}\bigg|_{\varepsilon=s}\,ds.

Therefore

V_e(H) - V(0)
=
-\int_0^H p^*(t)^T \Delta_e\,dt + R_e,

where

R_e = \int_0^1 (1-s) \frac{d^2J^*}{d\varepsilon^2}\bigg|_{\varepsilon=s}\,ds.

Under (C1)–(C3), (B1), and the assumption that the Hessian of the Hamiltonian is uniformly bounded on the reachable tube, there exists C_R>0 such that

|R_e| \le C_R \,\kappa_e^{\text{tube}\,2},

with

C_R = \frac{1}{2}\sup_{\varepsilon\in[0,1]}\left\|\frac{d^2J^*}{d\varepsilon^2}\right\|.

The constant C_R depends explicitly on L_{zz} (the Lipschitz constant of the Hessian of the Hamiltonian), \|\psi_z^{-1}\|, and the tube geometry.

