Appendix D: Active-Set Regime — Control Constraints

D.1 Stechlinski Framework

Consider the case where only control constraints g(u)\le 0 are active. Under linear independence of the active constraints and strong second-order sufficiency, Stechlinski [3, Theorem 4.2] establishes that the parametric dependence of the solution is lexicographically smooth (implying Lipschitzian), and that sensitivity jumps resulting from active-set changes are characterized via an auxiliary nonsmooth sensitivity system obtained by lexicographic directional differentiation.

D.2 Directional Derivative Formula

Let \mathcal{A}\subseteq[0,H] denote the interval on which g(u^*)=0. Let \mu(t) denote the multiplier associated with the active constraint. The directional derivative of the value function in the direction \Delta_e is

DV_e(H;\Delta_e)
=
\int_{\mathcal{A}} \mu(t)^T \nabla_u g(u^*(t))\,\delta u_e(t)\,dt
+
\int_{\mathcal{A}} p(t)^T \Delta_e(t)\,dt
+
\cdots,

where \delta u_e solves the linearized sensitivity system

\dot{\delta x} = A(t)\delta x + B(t)\delta u - \Delta_e,\qquad
\delta x(0)=0,

and the constraint linearization

\nabla_u g(u^*(t))\,\delta u_e(t) = 0 \quad\text{on }\mathcal{A}.

The first term captures the change in the active set; the second captures the direct effect of the removed coupling.

D.3 Jump Estimate

The jump in V_e when the active set changes is bounded by

|\Delta V_e|
\le
\int_{\mathcal{A}} \|\mu(t)\|\,\|\nabla_u g(u^*(t))\|\,\|\delta u_e(t)\|\,dt
+
\int_{\mathcal{A}} \|p(t)\|\,\|\Delta_e(t)\|\,dt.

The multiplier \mu is nonzero only on \mathcal{A}. The projection of \Delta_e onto the normal cone of the active constraint determines whether the perturbation activates or deactivates the constraint.

---

Appendix E: Active-Set Regime — State Constraints

E.1 Bettiol–Frankowska–Vinter Framework

For state constraints g(x)\le 0, the multiplier \mu is a measure supported on the active set \mathcal{A}. Bettiol, Frankowska, and Vinter [4] establish the following sensitivity relations:

Partial sensitivity relation.

p(t) \in \partial_C V(t,x^*(t)),

where \partial_C denotes the Clarke subdifferential with respect to the state variable.

Full sensitivity relation.

(p(t), -H(t,x^*(t),u^*(t),p(t))) \in \partial_C V(t,x^*(t)),

where \partial_C now denotes the Clarke subdifferential with respect to both time and state.

E.2 Directional Derivative Formula

The directional derivative of the value function in the direction \Delta_e is

DV_e(H;\Delta_e)
=
\min_{\mu\in\mathcal{M}}\max_{p\in\partial_C V}
\left[
\int_{\mathcal{A}} \mu^T \nabla_x g(x^*)\,\delta x_e\,dt
+
\int_{\mathcal{A}} p^T \Delta_e\,dt
\right],

where \delta x_e solves

\dot{\delta x} = A(t)\delta x - \Delta_e,\qquad \delta x(0)=0,

and \mathcal{M} is the set of admissible multipliers. The min–max structure reflects the nonsmoothness of the value function at active state constraints.

E.3 Jump Estimate

The jump in V_e is bounded by

|\Delta V_e|
\le
\sup_{\mu\in\mathcal{M}}\int_{\mathcal{A}} \|\mu(t)\|\,\|\nabla_x g(x^*(t))\|\,\|\delta x_e(t)\|\,dt
+
\sup_{p\in\partial_C V}\int_{\mathcal{A}} \|p(t)\|\,\|\Delta_e(t)\|\,dt.

The projection of \Delta_e onto the normal cone of the active state constraint determines the activation/deactivation behavior.

