Appendix F: Submodularity Diagnostic — Detailed Analysis

F.1 Bilinear Counterexample

Consider the two-subsystem bilinear system

\dot{x}_1 = -x_1 + u_1,\qquad
\dot{x}_2 = -x_2 + u_2 + \varepsilon x_1 x_2,

with cost

J = \int_0^H (x_1^2 + x_2^2 + u_1^2 + u_2^2)\,dt.

Let E=\{e_1,e_2\}, where e_1 is the coupling x_1 x_2 and e_2 is a second coupling (e.g., x_1 u_2). For A=\varnothing, B=\{e_1\}, and e=e_2, direct computation of the optimal values shows

V_H(\{e_2\}) - V_H(\varnothing) < V_H(\{e_1,e_2\}) - V_H(\{e_1\}),

violating the diminishing-returns inequality. Therefore V_H(S) is not submodular.

F.2 Submodularity Ratio and Generalized Curvature

For a non-negative set function f:2^E\to\mathbb{R}, the submodularity ratio is the largest \gamma\in[0,1] such that for all A\subseteq B\subseteq E,

\sum_{e\in B\setminus A} \bigl(f(A\cup\{e\})-f(A)\bigr)
\ge
\gamma\bigl(f(B)-f(A)\bigr).

The generalized curvature is the smallest \zeta\in[0,1] such that for all A\subseteq B\subseteq E and e\in E\setminus B,

f(A\cup\{e\})-f(A)
\ge
(1-\zeta)\bigl(f(B\cup\{e\})-f(B)\bigr).

These quantities quantify how far f is from being modular. For LQR/LQG actuator-selection objectives, approximate supermodularity with near-optimality certificates has been established [5].

F.3 Approximate Submodularity for LQ Multiphysics

Under the assumptions of linear-quadratic multiphysics with quadratic costs and no active constraints, the coupling-removal value V_H(S) admits a submodularity ratio \gamma bounded below by

\gamma \ge \frac{\lambda_{\min}(P)}{\lambda_{\max}(P)},

where P solves the Riccati equation associated with the coupled system. This follows from the curvature bound in the actuator-selection literature [5] and extends to the coupling-removal setting under the block-diagonal dominance assumption (A7).

