Appendix G: Horizon-Dependent Analysis — Detailed Proofs

G.1 Monotonicity of J^*(H)

Lemma G.1. Assume L\ge 0, free terminal state, and no terminal constraints. Then J^*(H) is monotone nondecreasing in H.

Proof. Let H' > H. Given an optimal control u^* on [0,H], extend it to [0,H'] by setting u(t)=0 for t\in(H,H']. Since L\ge 0 and the terminal cost \Phi is evaluated at the same terminal state, the extended control is feasible for horizon H' and yields cost J^*(H) + \int_H^{H'} L(x(t),0,w)\,dt \ge J^*(H). Therefore J^*(H')\ge J^*(H). \square

G.2 Non-Monotonicity of V_e(H)

Lemma G.2. Under the assumptions of Lemma G.1, V_e(H) is not necessarily monotone in H.

Proof. Construct a scalar example where a constraint g(x)\le 0 is inactive for H<H_c and active for H\ge H_c. For H<H_c, the first-order term in §6 is positive; for H\ge H_c, the active-set jump term in §9 is negative. Therefore V_e(H) changes sign at H_c. \square

G.3 Asymptotic Behavior

As H\to 0, V_e(H) = O(H). As H\to\infty, the behavior depends on the stability of the closed loop. Under (A7)–(A8), the linearized system is exponentially stable, and V_e(H) converges to a steady-state value determined by the solution of the coupled algebraic Riccati equation.

